WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.740
A few moments ago,
before I walked onstage,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.300
I was aware that there was this story
playing out in my head.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.020
It was actually a dance
between two stories.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.620
One story was noticing
the size of this audience

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.220
and the cameras that are around,

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.860
and was telling me that now would not be
a good time to trip over a shoelace

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.340
or forget any lines.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.340
And the other story in my head
was telling me to be grateful

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.700
for this opportunity.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.780
Go out, relish the experience,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.060
and have fun.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.780
I was aware that whichever story took hold

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.260
might impact the next
12 minutes of my life

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.940
and determine how this talk unfolds.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.060
Therapists and psychologists tell us
that the stories we tell ourselves

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.540
play a fundamental role in how we interact
with the world around us.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.180
Some people tell themselves
that they are not enough.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.020
Other people tell themselves
that they're impostors.

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.820
And if you've ever seen the auditions
of a television singing competition,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.260
some people tell themselves
they're a little better

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.980
than they actually are.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.100
But apart from the individual stories
we tell ourselves,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.100
all of us are also taking part
in larger, collective stories

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.140
that shape our behavior as a species.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:23.180
A collective story enables people in India
to treat cows as sacred,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:26.180
while people in America
cram cows into feedlots.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.300
Our internal and external worlds
are full of stories

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.020
that shape our behaviors.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.260
Today, I want to talk
about a collective story

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.180
that most people aren't even aware
they're inhabiting.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.500
It is a collective story that tells us
that human beings are separate

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.220
and superior to nature.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:52.860
It is a story that has taken us so close
to the brink of an unimaginable crisis

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:56.020
that our survival now depends
on telling a new story.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.900
So ...

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.220
Once upon a time, in a land far away --

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.740
and right here, actually,
on the lands that we're gathered today,

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.700
the majority of people on planet Earth

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.580
had a deep reverence
and respect for nature.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.460
They saw very little separation

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.900
between themselves
and the world around them.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.980
Many cultures saw nature
as a giving parent.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.980
The plants and the animals
were their relatives.

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.980
Indigenous Australians saw themselves
as custodians of the land,

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.300
while the ancient Chinese

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.620
considered themselves
reverent guests of nature.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.020
Even prominent figures of Rome,
like Ovid and Seneca,

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.420
argued that mining
shouldn't be permissible,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.100
as it was too abusive
to the natural world.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:49.300
But then, things started to change.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.100
A new craze called Christianity
began to take hold.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.660
Word spread that their one God
sat above and outside nature,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.220
and that God had made people
in his own image

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.860
and given them dominion over the Earth.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.580
"Sounds alright," said the people,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.780
passing on the news
to friends and relatives,

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.420
missionaries, kings.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.020
"The world was made for the sake of man,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.940
that it may serve him,"

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.540
said the bishop of Paris
in the early 12th century.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.340
The Christian craze
had now infiltrated Europe

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.780
and was beginning its march
into the Americas.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.700
"But wait, there's more,"

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.180
said a couple of fancy-looking men
in the early 1600s.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.180
They told the people that they were
from the Scientific Revolution,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.820
and that they could empower
the people even further.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.780
"We must hound nature in her wanderings,"

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.180
said the first man --

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.060
Francis Bacon,
the father of modern science.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.700
"We must find a way, at length,
into her inner chambers.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.780
We must reveal the secrets
still locked in nature's bosom.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.180
Make her your slave,

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.500
subdue her,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.700
shake her to her foundations."

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.340
"Yes," said the men. "It sounds alright."

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.700
The second man then chimed in.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.580
His name was René Descartes,
the father of modern philosophy.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.500
He confirmed to the people that, yes,

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.500
they were superior to nature

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.300
and added that animals
were mindless machines to be mastered

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.020
and exploited at will.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.020
"Well, that's a relief," said the people.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.860
"Now, we don't have to feel so bad
about whipping our oxen."

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.140
The influence of these two men,

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.980
coupled with the religious craze,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:42.540
meant that nature was no longer
seen as a living thing

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.020
to be revered and respected,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.940
but instead as a machine to be manipulated
for the benefits of mankind.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.740
This was a new and exciting story

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:59.540
that set free natural resources for humans
to achieve social and economic progress,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.660
even if obtained through violent
and suppressive acts.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:09.060
This new story was particularly enticing
to the emerging capitalists of the time,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.700
because a nature devoid
of reverence and respect

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.660
was much easier to commodify.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.820
And commodify they did.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.460
The humans went to work,

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.340
entering and penetrating
all of those inner chambers.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.180
They tunneled her bosom
for coal and metals,

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.740
they scraped and plowed
over her skin with their tractors.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.180
They took chain saws
to her forests of follicles,

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:36.420
and they filled her waters
with their waste.

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.260
The new story had spread across the globe.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:42.940
Humans had asserted their dominance,

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.420
they built wondrous things

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:47.620
and improved the lives
of billions of people,

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.820
particularly in specific regions.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:53.860
Until one day ...

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.540
their scientists began to notice.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.940
"Her animals are decreasing."

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.140
"Her atmosphere is heating."

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.580
"The soils are eroding."

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.500
"Our research is foreboding."

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:17.780
But still, the humans carried on.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:21.860
You see, they couldn't hear
the scientists,

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.900
because the facts don't matter much
if they don't fit the story.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.260
And the story was deeply embedded now.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.140
In 2019, the head of the Environmental
Protection Agency in the US

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:38.660
opposes regulations because, he says,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.540
"We have a responsibility to harvest

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.420
the natural resources
that we've been blessed with."

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.860
A prominent evangelist tells his followers

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.700
that refusing to use fossil fuels
hurts God's feelings.

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.460
The story was so embedded

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.500
that the humans' nightly news bulletins
measured their success

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.500
by financial metrics alone,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:03.500
while the living world that allowed
the gains in those metrics

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.860
was being eviscerated out of sight.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:09.700
And the story was so embedded

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.620
that when researchers looked
at the names of trees, birds, flowers

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.180
and other keywords relating to nature,

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:19.260
used across millions
of books, songs and movies,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.500
from 1900 to 2014 ...

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.180
they found a dramatic decline
in the use of those words

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:26.740
across that period.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:32.940
The humans were spending
seven hours a day on their screens.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.380
So not only were they experiencing

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:40.660
fewer stories and actual
experiences of nature ...

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:47.020
but they were being bombarded
by up to 10,000 advertisements a day,

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:51.060
largely for products that are inflicting
even more ecological damage.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:56.540
Nature was being hounded
in her wanderings.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.380
And yet, the humans remain
trapped in their story.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.660
Trapped in their cultural programming ...

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.940
like goldfish in a tank,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:12.500
unaware of the unfolding chaos
beyond the colored pebbles

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.180
and the artificial logs.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:19.820
So when those same scientists
resorted to blocking traffic

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.140
and tying themselves to poles

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.500
or screaming that the Amazon rainforest,

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.940
the most spectacular of all her forests,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.380
was nearing a tipping point
that would turn her into a savanna ...

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.700
still, the humans did nothing.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.860
Because to them, those trees ...

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:42.540
those trees that were home
to thousands of species of animals

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.740
and millions of species of insects,

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.060
those trees that sent
nutrients to each other

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.420
via underground fungal networks ...

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:55.100
those trees that transpired moisture
into the air to create rainfall

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.860
that would feed crops in countries
thousands of kilometers away ...

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.900
those trees were just ...

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.420
timber for decking.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.100
Pulp for toilet paper.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.100
Or space for more cows.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:19.020
Those trees were worth more
dead than alive ...

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:23.300
because that's what the story
had told them.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:27.620
But then, something remarkable happened.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.540
It started with the children,

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.780
who began to skip school
and take to the streets.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.420
It started with the farmers
who chose to stop fighting nature

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.580
and instead rebuild their soils.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.740
It started when the Indigenous people,

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.780
who, for centuries, had been reminding
everybody of their story,

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.420
were finally being listened to.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.780
And it started when nature herself,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:57.660
through fires and storms,

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.580
through droughts and rising waters,

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.060
forced her way back
into the people's lives

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.900
and demanded their respect.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:10.420
A new, regenerative story
about human beings and nature

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:11.580
was emerging.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.660
But of course,
it wasn't a new story at all.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.820
It was the retelling of an old story.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:22.580
But this time,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.420
the old story was supported
by the science.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:27.820
And it was telling the people

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:30.580
that every breath they took
was dependent on trees

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.660
and phytoplankton,

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.380
And that trillions of bacteria and fungi
lived on them and in them

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:37.980
and kept them alive.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.420
Viewing the natural world
as separate to humans

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.180
was now empirically false.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.060
Humans are nature.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:52.700
But the science was also telling them
that plants could see,

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.740
they could smell, hear ...

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:58.140
they could learn and store memories.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.780
That dolphins gossiped
and spoke in local dialects,

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.420
elephants held ceremonies
for dead relatives,

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:08.380
grasshoppers could turn into locusts
and back again in a matter of hours,

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.140
and that termites had built
an underground metropolis

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.220
the size of the United Kingdom.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:19.540
The same scientific inquiry that had led
to domination and extraction

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:23.860
had gone so deep into nature's bosom
that it was revealing her secrets.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:29.300
And her secrets were divulging
that she was anything but mechanistic.

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:33.980
That she was deserving
of the utmost reverence and respect.

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:39.660
And that the original story
had been right all along.

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:44.820
And so perhaps now,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:50.020
the humans would no longer refer to nature
as an unruly female to be tamed

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.420
or an "it" to be exploited ...

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.660
but would refer to nature simply ...

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:58.860
as kin.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:08.900
Nobody knows how this new
but old story ends

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.700
because it is still being written.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:15.460
But if it is to have the Hollywood ending,

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.060
if we are to break free
from our cultural programming

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:21.860
and pull off the miraculous comeback
when all seems lost,

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:25.940
then the new but old story will have to be
rapidly spread throughout the culture.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.780
It will need to be embedded
into all levels of curriculum,

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.780
particularly at the early stages,

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.860
so children can see the world
as a living system,

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.900
not as a machine
full of stocks and commodities.

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:42.300
It will need to inform
a redesign of the economy,

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.580
so it values nature

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.380
and reflects the true environmental costs
of the materials we all use.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:52.780
It will need to revamp
the nightly news bulletins

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.620
so we'd measure soil health,
atmospheric pollution, species loss

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.780
alongside the financial metrics.

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:01.580
And crucially,

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:03.980
the new but old story
will need to be amplified

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:05.700
by the storytellers.

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:09.300
The musicians, the artists,

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:11.500
those that can create
the emotional connection

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.300
to the living world once again,

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.780
and paint visions
of a nature-filled future

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:19.620
that people can see
and feel and strive for.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.340
Because stories shape culture.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.300
Culture shapes leaders,

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:31.900
leaders shape policies,
and policies shape the system.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.300
And perhaps,

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.780
just perhaps, one day ...

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:41.900
a few hundred years from now ...

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:48.500
historians will look back to this moment,
and they'll see that,

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.860
amongst the chaos and the nihilism
and the fear and the extinctions

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.900
that there were groups of people
who chose to turn the page

00:13:54.940 --> 00:13:57.300
and begin to write
a new chapter for humanity.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:03.580
A chapter full of diverse characters,
from a range of professions and places,

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.780
who came together

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:07.940
to create a thriving,

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.620
regenerative,

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.100
ecological future.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.500
The end.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:21.180
(Cheers and applause)

